("Money map and goals","Basic","🧭","Decide what money must do before picking a product.",[("Goals","Name a purpose and time horizon","Next month's bills vs retirement"),("Priority","Protect essentials before optional spending","Rent and groceries first"),("Review","Revisit after income or life changes","Monthly money check-in")],"Sort goals into near-term needs and long-term plans.","Is the highest-return investment always the first step?","No. Cash needs, costly debt, risk and goals may come first.","Write three goals with dates and rough amounts.",["CFPB financial toolkit","SEC investing basics"],["Name goal","Set horizon","Choose priority","Review"]),
("Income and cash flow","Basic","💵","Money in minus money out shows whether a plan can continue.",[("Net income","Cash received after withholding","Take-home paycheck"),("Fixed costs","Bills that recur predictably","Housing and insurance"),("Variable costs","Amounts that change","Groceries and travel")],"If take-home pay is $5,000 and outflows are $4,600, $400 remains for goals and surprises.","Is a positive annual salary enough to avoid cash shortages?","No. Timing and recurring outflows determine available cash.","Review two or three months of actual transactions.",["CFPB spending"],["Income","Outflows","Difference","Adjust"]),
("Spending and budgeting","Basic","🧾","Track real categories, then choose one manageable change.",[("Needs","Core recurring obligations","Housing and food"),("Wants","Flexible spending","Subscriptions or dining out"),("Irregular bills","Nonmonthly but foreseeable","Annual registration")],"Set aside a monthly amount for a $600 annual bill rather than treating it as a surprise.","Must every household use a fixed 50/30/20 split?","No. A budget should reflect actual obligations and goals.","Categorize last month's transactions and one annual bill.",["CFPB spending","CFPB financial toolkit"],["Track","Group","Plan","Review"]),
("Emergency fund and cash safety","Basic","🛟","Keep accessible reserves for unplanned expenses.",[("Purpose","Buffer against disruptions","Repair or lost income"),("Liquidity","Access money when needed","Insured savings account"),("Coverage","Understand account ownership rules","FDIC ownership category")],"Build a small first buffer and expand based on job stability, dependents and essential costs.","Is an emergency fund the same as a stock portfolio?","No. Emergency money should be reliably available when needed.","Choose an initial cash target and check deposit coverage.",["CFPB emergency fund","FDIC deposit insurance"],["Set buffer","Automate","Check coverage","Reassess"]),
("Assets, liabilities and net worth","Basic","⚖️","Net worth equals assets minus liabilities; liquidity still matters.",[("Assets","Things you own with financial value","Cash, investments, home"),("Liabilities","Amounts you owe","Mortgage and credit balance"),("Net worth","Snapshot difference","Assets minus debts")],"A $600k home with a $400k mortgage contributes $200k in home equity before transaction costs.","Does a high net worth mean all bills can be paid immediately?","No. A home may be valuable but illiquid.","List each asset and debt with a date and estimate.",["CFPB financial toolkit","CFPB mortgages"],["List assets","List debts","Subtract","Check liquidity"]),
("Credit reports and scores","Intermediate","🪪","Review credit information and pay on time; a score is only one signal.",[("Report","Record of borrowing history","Check for errors"),("Payment","Late payments can cost fees and harm credit","Calendar reminder"),("Utilization","Revolving balance relative to limit","Review card statement")],"Inspect reports and dispute an account that is not yours through the official process.","Does checking your own credit report hurt your score?","No. Checking your own report is not a hard inquiry.","Review your reports and set payment reminders.",["CFPB credit reports"],["Get report","Check entries","Dispute errors","Maintain habits"]),
("Debt, interest and payoff","Intermediate","💳","Compare APR, minimum payment and payoff time before borrowing.",[("APR","Annualized cost of borrowing","Card at 20% APR"),("Principal","Balance owed","Remaining loan balance"),("Payoff plan","Extra payment can shorten time and interest","Fixed monthly payment")],"A high-rate card balance can grow if payments barely cover monthly interest.","Does paying only the minimum always clear debt quickly?","No. It can take much longer and cost more interest.","List balances, APRs and required payments.",["CFPB debt","CFPB financial toolkit"],["List debt","Compare APR","Plan payment","Track balance"]),
("Housing and major purchases","Intermediate","🏠","Compare total cost, cash needs and flexibility, not just the monthly payment.",[("Upfront cost","Down payment, closing and moving","Cash at purchase"),("Ongoing cost","Loan, tax, insurance, upkeep","Monthly ownership budget"),("Tradeoff","Money tied in a home has alternatives","Other goals and flexibility")],"A lower mortgage payment may still come with taxes, insurance and maintenance.","Is mortgage payment the whole housing cost?","No. Include taxes, insurance, maintenance and transaction costs.","Make a complete cost table before a major purchase.",["CFPB mortgages","CFPB spending"],["Upfront","Monthly","Risks","Decision"]),
("Investing foundations","Intermediate","📈","Match investment risk to goal horizon and ability to tolerate loss.",[("Stock","Ownership in a company","One share"),("Bond","Debt security with its own risks","Bond fund"),("Cash","Stable access but inflation tradeoff","Savings for near-term need")],"Money needed next year may need different risk than money intended for retirement decades away.","Can an investment with higher expected return be risk-free?","No. Return and risk are related; outcomes are uncertain.","Write the time horizon for each investment account.",["SEC investing basics","SEC allocation"],["Horizon","Risk","Allocation","Review"]),
("Funds, diversification and allocation","Intermediate","🧺","Diversification reduces single-holding risk but does not prevent market losses.",[("Fund","A basket of holdings","Broad index fund"),("Allocation","Mix across stocks, bonds and cash","Long-term target"),("Rebalance","Restore chosen proportions","Review periodically")],"A broad fund can spread exposure across many firms while remaining exposed to overall market risk.","Does owning many shares of one company diversify a portfolio?","No. It remains concentrated in that one company.","List asset mix and largest single holding.",["SEC allocation","SEC investing basics"],["Choose mix","Diversify","Check concentration","Rebalance"]),
("Stocks and employer equity","Intermediate","🏢","Company stock can add concentrated risk when pay and investments depend on the same employer.",[("Single stock","Company-specific uncertainty","Earnings surprise"),("Employer equity","Compensation tied to employer","RSUs or ESPP shares"),("Tax lots","Purchase/grant basis and dates matter","Record each lot")],"If salary and a large portfolio position depend on one firm, one adverse event can affect both.","Does a discount on employer stock remove its future price risk?","No. Discount and future market risk are separate; tax rules vary by plan.","Measure employer-stock share of investable assets and keep plan records.",["SEC allocation","IRS investments tax"],["Get shares","Record basis","Check share","Plan risk"]),
